+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,159 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy
+
+Fizzy ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) configuration for a
+single-box deployment: one Linux server running Docker, SQLite for the
+database, local disk storage for uploads, and automatic HTTPS from
+`kamal-proxy`. Background jobs run inside the web process via Solid Queue, so
+there is nothing else to install or operate.
+
+To deploy, you need:
+
+* A Linux server with Docker installed and reachable over SSH.
+* A domain name whose DNS `A`/`AAAA` record points at that server.
+* Ports `80` and `443` open so Let's Encrypt and HTTPS can work.
+* Docker on the machine you deploy from. Kamal builds the image locally and
+  transfers it through a temporary local registry, so you do not need an
+  account on Docker Hub or another registry.
+
+## 1. Fill in your server and hostname
+
+Edit [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml):
+
+* `servers.web` — the IP address or hostname Kamal should SSH into.
+* `proxy.host` — the public hostname Fizzy will be served from.
+* `env.clear.APP_FQDN` — the same public hostname. Fizzy uses it to build
+  links in email and notifications.
+
+If your server does not allow SSH as `root`, add an SSH section:
+
+```yaml
+ssh:
+  user: deploy
+```
+
+The referenced user needs permission to run Docker (usually `sudo` access or
+membership in the `docker` group). Kamal's server bootstrap can install Docker
+for you on a fresh box.
+
+## 2. Fill in your mail server
+
+Emails are required for magic-link sign-in, so Fizzy needs an SMTP server it
+can send through. Update these values in `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+```yaml
+env:
+  clear:
+    MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS: "Fizzy <fizzy@your-domain.com>"
+    SMTP_ADDRESS: smtp.your-provider.com
+    SMTP_PORT: "587"
+    SMTP_USERNAME: fizzy@your-domain.com
+    SMTP_AUTHENTICATION: plain
+    SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO: "true"
+```
+
+The SMTP password is a secret and belongs in the next step. These settings
+are also available if your provider needs them:
+
+| Variable | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `SMTP_DOMAIN` | The HELO/EHLO domain. Defaults to `APP_FQDN`. |
+| `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` | `plain` by default; use `login` if your provider expects it. |
+| `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO` | STARTTLS on port 587. `"true"` by default. |
+| `SMTP_SSL` | Set to `"true"` for implicit TLS, usually on port 465. |
+| `SMTP_TLS` | Set to `"true"` only if your provider documents it. |
+| `SMTP_OPENSSL_VERIFY_MODE` | OpenSSL verify mode, e.g. `peer`. |
+
+## 3. Set your secrets
+
+Kamal reads secret values from [`.kamal/secrets`](../.kamal/secrets). There
+are two ways to provide the application secret; pick one:
+
+**Option A: `SECRET_KEY_BASE`.** This is the simplest one-box setup. Generate
+a stable secret once:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails secret
+```
+
+Then export it in the shell where you run Kamal, along with your SMTP
+password:
+
+```sh
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE="<paste the generated secret here>"
+export SMTP_PASSWORD="<your SMTP password>"
+```
+
+Alternatively, replace the `$SECRET_KEY_BASE` and `$SMTP_PASSWORD`
+references in `.kamal/secrets` with literal values.
+
+**Option B: Rails credentials.** Run:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails credentials:edit
+```
+
+This creates `config/master.key`, which `.kamal/secrets` reads
+automatically as `RAILS_MASTER_KEY`. Rails also stores a generated
+`secret_key_base` in the encrypted credentials, so you can leave
+`SECRET_KEY_BASE` unset.
+
+In both cases, set `SMTP_PASSWORD` in your shell (or put the literal value in
+`.kamal/secrets`), and do not commit real secrets to the repository.
+
+Your application secret must stay the same across deploys; it signs sessions
+and secure tokens. Keep it somewhere safe along with your SMTP password.
+
+## 4. Deploy
+
+From the repository checkout, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This bootstraps the server, builds the image, starts `kamal-proxy` with an
+automatically issued TLS certificate, creates the SQLite databases, and starts
+Fizzy. When it finishes, visit `https://your-hostname` and sign in with your
+email address.
+
+## Data and backups
+
+All state lives under `/rails/storage` in the container and is mounted on the
+named Docker volume `fizzy_storage`:
+
+* `production.sqlite3` — Fizzy accounts, boards, cards, etc.
+* `production_queue.sqlite3` — Solid Queue background jobs.
+* `production_cache.sqlite3` — Solid Cache.
+* `production_cable.sqlite3` — Solid Cable.
+* `production/files` — uploaded files.
+
+`bin/kamal deploy` starts a new container with the same volume, so your data
+survives redeploys. To take a backup, run a SQLite backup inside the running
+container and copy it off the server:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal shell
+sqlite3 storage/production.sqlite3 ".backup '/rails/storage/backup.sqlite3'"
+exit
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse "cat /rails/storage/backup.sqlite3" > fizzy-backup.sqlite3
+```
+
+## Background jobs
+
+There is no separate jobs container. `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA` is enabled in
+`config/deploy.yml`, so Solid Queue's dispatchers and workers run in the same
+Puma process that serves web requests. Recurring jobs (notification bundles,
+cleanup, etc.) are scheduled by Solid Queue from `config/recurring.yml`.
+
+## Updating
+
+After pulling new code, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+The database migrations run automatically through the container entrypoint.
+Old containers and images are pruned by Kamal; the `fizzy_storage` volume is
+never removed, including by `bin/kamal remove` (remove it explicitly with
+`docker volume rm fizzy_storage` only when you are certain).
